                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1] An excellent introduction to shmups [/h1]\r\n[i]Jamestown[/i] is a fine enough game on its own, but the real value of this game is in how it teaches you to play, and appreciate, shmups.\r\n\r\nIt's cleverly designed so that you tackle the game level-by-level, with each stage being a self-contained challenge.  Once you've cleared all the levels on their own, you unlock the \"Gauntlet\" mode, where you have to play through each level one after the other with just a few continues.  The goal, of course, is to clear the Gauntlet without continuing.  In effect, it teaches you the process (and joy) of breaking a shmup down into smaller parts to practice it, until you get good enough to string it all together and get that one-credit clear.\r\n\r\nIt's an experience that you can bring to other shmups and arcade games in general.  As a means of gaining an appreciation for this kind of gaming, [i]Jamestown[/i] is second to none.\r\n\r\nIt definitely helps that the game looks and sounds so good.  The backgrounds and sprites are all excellently drawn and very reminiscent of the Metal Slug games.  You have several ships to choose from, and they all play very differently.  If you can get into the game, there's a lot to do.\r\n\r\nAs a shump, it's alright.  Clearing the stages and then working your way up to clearing the Gauntlet mode without continuing is pretty enjoyable.  The biggest issue with the game is its aspect ratio.  In a shmup, you generally want to be scrolling in the direction of the greatest screen real estate; this is why horizontal-oriented shooters scroll horizontally, while vertical-oriented shooters scroll vertically.\r\n\r\nAlthough [i]Jamestown[/i] is a vertically scrolling shmup, it has a pseudo-widescreen aspect ratio, and doesn't feel like this was actually taken into consideration in designing the stages.  You are often overwhelmed by enemies and bullets on your sides, while struggling with what feels like limited visibility ahead of you.  It's nothing that cannot be overcome with a little practice, but it makes the game a bit uncomfortable to play.\r\n\r\nHowever, this is a minor issue and the game is definitely worth playing.  Who knows?  Playing it might even get you interested in getting 1CCs for other shumps and arcade games.",
